  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A fair $8$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $8$ is rolled $12$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ or $8$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =1.732\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
